"""Every agent that helps with Olo, each a child tab of the lead's own
Rex tab (started, read, answered and stopped through `olo agent`: Rex's
own support for an agent's children, which needs no desktop; Ben,
2026-11-26: "when you use subagents for review/qa please use the new
olo/rex native subagent support you built"), or, where the lead isn't in
a Rex pane, a tmux session of its own as all of them were until then:
narrow, parallel work items, second investigators and the looks at a
change's pictures. Each is an interactive Antigravity CLI (`agy`: Gemini
4 Neon, its reasoning at xhigh), the look at pictures for what is wrong
by a pixel too, but the design review, Opus 5.5 at max in the Claude CLI
(Ben, 2026-11-25: "NOTHING should still be using fable -- everything
should be either opus 5.5 max or neon 4 xhigh"); all are read, answered and steered from here
(CLAUDE.md, "Subagents"; the olo-agents and olo-debug-help skills say
how to use them). Ben, 2026-11-22: "stop using codex/astra for olo
qa/dev tasks and … replace it with antigravity (`agy`) and gemini 4
neon on xhigh", run "via tmux … so that you will get notified when it
needs you and be able to steer it and approve things", with "the auto
approval stuff [configured] to auto approve all the most common
commands it might need to run"; and of every helper, the reviewers
too: "always do it through tmux never a script that just goes silent".

  agents start NAME CRATE BRIEF.md   start a worker on CRATE (a folder, apps/updates; or a
                                     file or folder that isn't a crate, data/words/de.txt)
                                     with the task in BRIEF.md; at most 5 at once
  agents investigate BRIEF.md [DIR]  start a second investigator on the bug in
                                     BRIEF.md (tools/dev/agy-help is this), its
                                     answer written to DIR (the brief's folder),
                                     and wait as `watch` does, for it alone; run
                                     again, it goes on waiting for the same one
  agents look [--by opus] [--about TEXT] PICTURE…   (Neon's look by default)
                                     have a change's pictures looked at: by Gemini 4
                                     Neon at xhigh for what's wrong by a pixel
                                     (tools/dev/agy-look is this), or by Opus 5.5 at
                                     max for the design itself (--by opus:
                                     tools/dev/opus-look). The review is
                                     written beside the first picture and printed,
                                     waited for as `investigate` waits
  agents review [--about TEXT] [--tree DIR] [--dry-run] FROM..TO [PATH…]
  agents review [--about TEXT] [--tree DIR] [--dry-run] --working [PATH…]
                                     have a change's code reviewed by Astra 6.2 at
                                     max in the Codex CLI (tools/dev/astra-review is
                                     this): the commits of RANGE (`main~3..main`,
                                     `v2026.11.27.7..HEAD`), or with --working what
                                     isn't committed yet (PATHs: only those). It
                                     reads the diff and the tree (DIR: another
                                     checkout than this one), runs nothing, and
                                     writes its review under /var/tmp/olo-reviews;
                                     waited for and printed as a look is
  agents list                        each one: what it's on, its state (working, asking,
                                     idle: a question or waiting, done: its result
                                     written, exited, gone), when it was started
  agents watch [--stuck MIN] [--for MIN] [--only NAME[,NAME…]]
                                     return once one needs you: an approval asked,
                                     a turn finished (DONE, a question), gone, or
                                     nothing new for MIN minutes (15) while working.
                                     Run it in the background and re-run it after
                                     each answer: it's how you hear from them. It
                                     prints a line a minute for each meanwhile
                                     (what it is doing), to read as it goes. With
                                     --for, it returns after that long at most (for
                                     a minder, who waits on it in the foreground).
                                     --only: the agents whose names begin with one
                                     of those alone (`--only words-`: a minder's own)
  agents peek NAME [LINES]           the last LINES (60) of its screen
  agents log NAME                    its whole transcript
  agents say NAME [--now] TEXT...    a message to it: an answer, steering. While it
                                     works it is queued for the end of its step;
                                     --now delivers it at once (stops a long think)
  agents approve NAME [y|p|no]       answer what it asks leave for: yes this once (y),
                                     yes and don't ask again in this conversation for
                                     commands that start the same (p), or no (then
                                     `say` why); says if its screen didn't move on
                                     (status 4: look before answering again), or if
                                     the same is asked a second time (answer it too)
  agents answer NAME N               take the Nth choice of any other question it
                                     shows with choices
  agents keys NAME KEY…              press keys in it (up, down, enter, esc, tab, a
                                     digit, a letter): for a question whose choices
                                     have no numbers, or esc to stop what it's doing
  agents stop NAME                   end it, when you have what you need of it: its
                                     transcript is kept in its folder, and any test
                                     session it left (agy-NAME…) is stopped. (Its
                                     folder, /var/tmp/olo-agents/NAME, with its
                                     report, stays until `forget`; its builds a day
                                     more: below)
  agents resume NAME [--result FILE] [--session ID] TEXT…
                                     bring a stopped worker back with its
                                     conversation (a child tab: `olo agent resume`),
                                     told TEXT, with a minder as at its start; FILE
                                     is what it is to write this time (DONE is then
                                     that file's, not its last report's). For a
                                     second pass on its own work, after its review.
                                     Where the service has trimmed its record, it
                                     is started anew on its own conversation (by
                                     the id kept at its stop, or ID)
  agents tidy                        end what nobody needs any more (list, watch and
                                     start do this too): an agent whose program has
                                     exited, one done and untouched for half an hour,
                                     one idle for three hours; remove a folder whose
                                     agent ended over a week ago
  agents forget NAME                 remove a stopped one's folder
  agents attach NAME                 how the person can watch it live (read-only)
  agents allowed                     what an agent runs without asking, and what
                                     it is refused outright

A worker's folder holds brief.md (the rules, then the task), report.md
(what it says it did, when done), meta.json, settings.json (what it may
run without asking: below) and target/ (its own CARGO_TARGET_DIR, so its
builds never wait on another's). An investigator's answer.md and
notes.md go in its DIR, yours, with its builds in DIR/target. An agent
that has ended keeps its builds for a day after the last one: then
`list`, `watch` and `start` remove target/ (16 GB of one sat for nobody,
2026-11-21; kache doesn't know these folders, so nothing else would).
The rest of the folder stays until `forget`.

What needs you, and what doesn't. An agent runs in agy's accept-edits
mode: its edits to files go ahead, and so does every command that
starts as one of ALLOW does (reading, searching, building, testing,
git's reading commands, Olo's own test tools); a command line of
several (a pipe, `&&`) goes ahead when each of them does. What starts
as one of DENY does is refused without asking (anything that changes
git, sudo, a helper of its own started with this tool). Everything else
stops and asks, and is given leave wherever it is what the task
reasonably needs: refused is only what is dangerous, out of line with
the task, or better done a safer way (Ben, 2026-11-27: "you're
micromanaging these subagents way too much let them do whatever they
need to reasonably do to do their job. only deny dangerous commands
that are out of alignment with the task at hand or where they could be
done safer"). `watch` tells you of what asks: `approve` it, or say no. The lists are given to that one
run (`agy --settings`): the person's own agy settings are never read
for it nor written (the tool never takes the choice that would save a
rule there).

An agent can't start helpers of its own: OLO_SUBAGENT is set in its
environment, which this script and its front doors (agy-help, agy-look,
opus-look) refuse. The agent programs themselves (`claude`, `codex`,
`agy`) it may run where its task is about them: their `--help` and
`--version` go ahead unasked, anything else of theirs asks (a test of
how one reads a file is the task's; handing its own work to one is
not). They were refused outright until 2026-11-27, and a builder whose
task was the three programs' own instruction files could not read
their help.
